A||||8||mrfixit58||Adjusting cooling fan switch???||||5||
Z||000000||mrfixit58||11-13-2000||02:14 PM||||I notice that my car actually runs hotter with the A/C "off". It was a cool morning here (about 70ºf) and, while idling for about 5 minutes waiting for a train to pass, the temperature climbed to 220ºf. I flpped on the A/C and the temperture went down to 180ºf. <P>I reviewed the On-line Service Guide (http://www.geocities.com/MotorCity/Garage/5007/fiero_osg/coolant-fan-switch.html) and it said that the high temperature switch does not kick in until the motor reaches 235ºf! I think that's too high.<P>Does anyone know of a way to lower the switch "on" temperature? Say to about 220ºf? I don't really want to install a manual switch but, if no one has any neat tricks, I may.<P>Thanks,<BR>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||5
Z||000001||deceler8||11-13-2000||02:54 PM||||Get one of Rodney Dickman's 195 fan switches.<P>Or install the manual switch.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||11-13-2000||08:04 PM||||You can also get an adjustable one used for aux fan kits and tie that in up front. (These work on the radiator) You have to plan this out some or the switch won't last long. It doesn't like constant soaking with water.<P>there's also a couple article in my cave about the cooling system that may help you decide which way you want to go. <A HREF="http://fierocave.shorturl.com" TARGET=_blank>The Fiero Cave</A><p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 11-13-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||batboy||11-13-2000||08:43 PM||||I didn't know Rodney had a 195 degree switch. I've order stuff from him twice... high quality parts and one really nice guy. Here is another possible option.<P> <A HREF="http://store.summitracing.com/product.asp?ssid=61VJHCXVN2VWRY11IBX1S7PF4L&p=A6ED197F-3612-11D3-949D-080009FC5B5F" TARGET=_blank>http://store.summitracing.com/product.asp?ssid=61VJHCXVN2VWRY11IBX1S7PF4L&p=A6ED197F-3612-11D3-949D-080009FC5B5F</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||fiero56||11-13-2000||10:23 PM||||Batboy, do you have any experience with adjustable switch you linked to?(I know, bad english) <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Wondering how the adjustments are made, and where it hooks up.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Seanh||11-13-2000||10:58 PM||||I would reccomend a low temp fan switch as well as the lower temp thermostate from <A HREF="http://www.rodneydickman.com" TARGET=_blank>Rodney</A><P>------------------<BR><A HREF="http://www.kdsi.net/~sean" TARGET=_blank>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/seanhsig2.gif">     <BR>Sean's Fiero Page</A><BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8 Auto<BR>Going on to 3.4 in Jan.<BR><I>"If you smoke after sex, you're doing it way to fast".</I>||||reg||1
Z||000006||mrfixit58||11-14-2000||07:34 AM||||Thanks guys. I'll probably wind up ordering Rodney's switch (thanks for pointing it out). Ogre - I'll check your cave before I do.<P>Thanks again,<BR>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||14
Z||000007||batboy||11-14-2000||09:19 PM||||No experience with that switch. A forum member posted that he bought one of these about a year ago, but never heard afterwards how it worked out for him. Going with Rodney's switch is a sure thing. That's what I would and probably will do.||||reg||1
Z||000008||fiero56||11-14-2000||10:57 PM||||Since it has been snowing here on and off today, I don't have to worry about overheating right now, but I think I will try out that adjustable switch. I like the versatility it provides, and I can try differnt temps painlessly.||||reg||1
